MLLM description: The House Fly (Musca domestica) is a small to medium-sized fly, typically measuring between 6-8 mm in length. Its body is robust and compact, with a dark brown or black coloration. The thorax is covered with short, dense hairs, giving it a slightly fuzzy appearance. The abdomen is segmented and also has a dark coloration, often with lighter bands or stripes.

The wings are transparent with a slight golden tint near the base, and they are held flat against the body when at rest. The eyes are large and globular, with a reddish-brown hue, and they are positioned on either side of the head, allowing the fly to have excellent binocular vision. The legs are sturdy and hairy, aiding in walking and grasping surfaces.

Behaviorally, House Flies are known for their strong flying ability and their habit of landing on various surfaces, including food, plants, and human skin. They are attracted to sweet substances and are often found around garbage bins, fruit, and other decaying organic matter. 

In terms of identification, the House Fly can be distinguished by its overall shape, size, and coloration. It is one of the most common flies found worldwide and is easily recognizable due to its distinctive appearance.
